---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Eyebrow from '../components/Eyebrow.astro';
import CTA from '../components/CTA.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Get Started"
  description="Install ryft, pick a backend, and run your first StableHLO program."
>
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-[1100px] px-6 pt-16 pb-24">
    <div class="font-display uppercase tracking-[0.22em] text-xs text-[var(--color-ryft-fg-muted)] mb-4 flex items-center gap-3">
      <span class="block h-px w-8 bg-[color-mix(in_srgb,var(--color-ryft-fg)_25%,transparent)]"></span>
      <span>Get Started</span>
    </div>

    <h1
      class="font-serif font-semibold tracking-[-0.018em] leading-[1.05] text-[clamp(2rem,3vw_+_1rem,3rem)] text-[var(--color-ryft-fg)] mb-8 max-w-[20ch]"
    >
      From <code class="font-mono text-[0.92em] text-[var(--color-ryft-accent)]">cargo add</code> to a running StableHLO program.
    </h1>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Ryft is published on
        <a href="https://crates.io/crates/ryft">crates.io</a> as a workspace of
        small focused crates. The umbrella crate, <code>ryft</code>, re-exports
        the modules you usually want.
      </p>

      <Eyebrow>1 · Add ryft to your Cargo.toml</Eyebrow>

      <pre><code>{`[dependencies]
ryft = "0.0.3"`}</code></pre>

      <p>
        The default feature set enables both the <code>ndarray</code> backend
        (for quick iteration on CPU) and the <code>xla</code> backend (for the
        StableHLO / PJRT compiler path).
      </p>

      <Eyebrow>2 · Pick an accelerator (optional)</Eyebrow>

      <p>
        Accelerator-specific features are forwarded through the crate stack.
        Enable the one matching the device you want to run on:
      </p>

      <pre><code>{`# CUDA 13 (Linux x86_64 / arm64)
ryft = { version = "0.0.3", features = ["cuda-13"] }

# ROCm 7 (Linux x86_64)
ryft = { version = "0.0.3", features = ["rocm-7"] }

# Apple Metal (macOS arm64)
ryft = { version = "0.0.3", features = ["metal"] }`}</code></pre>

      <p>
        Without an accelerator feature flag, ryft uses the CPU PJRT plugin.
        Platform notes and artifact resolution details live in the per-crate
        READMEs — start with
        <a href="https://github.com/eaplatanios/ryft/blob/main/crates/ryft-xla-sys/README.md">
          <code>ryft-xla-sys/README.md</code>
        </a>.
      </p>

      <Eyebrow>3 · Run the StableHLO example</Eyebrow>

      <p>
        The repository ships a runnable end-to-end example that builds, compiles,
        and executes a `2x3 · 3x2` matrix multiplication through the
        <code>ryft::mlir</code> and <code>ryft::pjrt</code> APIs:
      </p>

      <pre><code>{`git clone https://github.com/eaplatanios/ryft
cd ryft
cargo run -p ryft --example stable_hlo_matmul`}</code></pre>

      <p>
        Expected output:
        <code>{`[58.0, 64.0, 139.0, 154.0]`}</code>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-12 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
      <CTA href="/examples/stable-hlo-matmul/" variant="primary">Read the example</CTA>
      <CTA href="/book/" variant="minimal">Open the book</CTA>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
